does this image show?\" but \"what is a learner supposed to learn from this image, and how do I carry that meaning in words?\"\n\nThis section treats accessibility as a design obligation that is decided when you choose the image, not bolted on at the end. It covers the three decisions that actually determine whether an image is accessible: classifying the image (decorative versus meaningful, which sets whether alt is empty or content-bearing), writing functional alt that carries the learning point concisely, and ensuring no meaning rides on color alone. Done well, accessibility is invisible and improves the design for everyone; done as an afterthought, it produces alt text that satisfies a checker and teaches nothing.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Classify an instructional image as decorative, informative, or functional and assign the correct alt treatment.\n- Write functional alt text that carries an image's instructional meaning, and move dense data to an adjacent table or long description.\n- Audit a visual for color-only coding and add a redundant cue per WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.1.\n- Apply the cover test and reject over-long AI-generated alt text with reasons.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA frame presents a bar chart comparing test scores across three teaching methods, with alt text that reads \"a colorful bar chart with blue, green, and orange bars.\" An automated accessibility checker passes it: the alt attribute is present and non-empty. Yet a screen-reader user learns nothing the chart was meant to teach, no ranking, no margin, no result, only that some colored bars exist. The checker measured presence; it could not measure function.\n\nWorse, the bars are distinguished by color alone, so a color-blind learner who can see the chart also cannot tell the series apart. The frame is \"accessible\" by automated audit and inaccessible in practice. This section asks you to expose that gap: passing a checker is not the goal, delivering the instructional meaning through a non-visual path is.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAccessible images turn on three distinct decisions, each with its own rule.\n\nFirst, **classification**. An image is either decorative (it adds no information the surrounding text lacks, for example a header rule or a stock photo of a campus) or meaningful (it carries content a learner must get). Decorative images take empty alt (`alt=\"\"`) so a screen reader skips them; announcing \"decorative divider line\" is noise that wastes a non-visual learner's attention. Meaningful images take content-bearing alt. Misclassifying in either direction harms: empty alt on a meaningful chart hides the content, verbose alt on decoration buries it.\n\nSecond, **function over appearance**. For a meaningful image, the alt must convey what the learner should take from it, the ranking, the relationship, the value, not the colors or layout. Compare \"blue, green, orange bars\" (appearance) with \"inquiry scored highest at 82, then lecture at 74, then lab at 70\" (function). Keep it brief; alt is read linearly with no skimming, so a complex chart's full data belongs in an adjacent table or a long description, with the alt giving the headline.\n\nThird, **do not encode meaning in color alone** (WCAG 2.2 success criterion 1.4.1, Use of Color; the alt obligation behind the first two decisions is SC 1.1.1, Non-text Content). If series are told apart only by hue, color-blind learners lose the distinction even with full sight; add a redundant cue, direct labels, patterns, or values on the bars. The operative conformance target as of 2026 is WCAG 2.2 AA, a W3C Recommendation since October 2023; WCAG 3.0 remains an early, exploratory W3C Working Draft that is still years from becoming a standard, so design and audit against 2.2.\n\nThese map onto the course anchors. UDL learner variability is the driver: design for the range of sensory and perceptual access, not the median learner (UDL Guidelines 3.0 frames sensory and perceptual access as ordinary learner variability to design for, not a special accommodation). Mayer's coherence principle keeps alt brief, because over-long alt is extraneous load delivered through the ear. And accessibility is a multimedia decision, not a compliance afterthought, so it belongs at image-selection time, beside the function and coherence decisions of 03.03.\n\n## Worked Example: Rewriting Alt Text for a Bar Chart\n\nA frame presents a bar chart comparing test scores across three teaching methods. Below is the before/after of its accessibility treatment.\n\n**Before (appearance, not function):**\n\n> alt = \"A colorful bar chart with blue, green, and orange bars on a white background.\" The series are distinguished by color only. No transcript.\n\nProblems: the alt text describes pixels, not the comparison the chart teaches. A screen-reader user learns nothing about the result, and a color-blind learner cannot tell the series apart.\n\n**After (function carried in words, color not load-bearing):**\n\n> alt = \"Bar chart: average score was highest for inquiry (82), then lecture (74), then lab (70); inquiry led by 8 points.\" Each bar is also labeled with its method name and value, so color is decorative, not the only cue. A short data table sits beside the chart.\n\nAnnotated decision trail:\n\n1. Rewrote alt to convey the instructional function (the ranking and the 8-point gap), not the colors.\n2. Removed color-only coding: added on-bar labels so the distinction survives color blindness and grayscale.\n3. Classified the image as meaningful, so it earns content-bearing alt, unlike the page's decorative header rule, which takes empty alt.\n4. Handled complexity: kept the alt brief and added a nearby data table for learners who need every value.\n5. Rejected suggestion: the AI proposed a 200-word alt attribute listing all values. Rejected, because alt should stay brief; the full data belongs in the adjacent table.\n\nThe improvement is access carried by structure, brief functional alt plus a readable equivalent, not a longer color description.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nRun every image through the accessibility sequence in order:\n\n1. **Classify:** Is this image decorative or meaningful? Decorative gets `alt=\"\"`. Meaningful gets content-bearing alt. If you are unsure, ask whether the surrounding text already conveys what the image conveys; if it does, the image is decorative.\n2. **Write to function, briefly:** For a meaningful image, state what the learner should take from it in one tight sentence. If the image is data-dense, give the headline in alt and put the full values in an adjacent table or long description.\n3. **Audit color:** Is any distinction carried by color alone? If yes, add a non-color cue (labels, values, patterns) so the meaning survives color blindness and grayscale, per WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.1.\n\nA defensible image decision can answer all three: how it was classified, what function its alt carries, and how it avoids color-only coding. The right test is the cover test: hide the image and read only the alt, can a learner still get the point?\n\nPut the accessibility decisions in the order this section runs them.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Classify: is the image decorative or meaningful?\",\"Decorative gets empty alt; meaningful earns content-bearing alt\",\"Write meaningful alt to function in one tight sentence, not to appearance\",\"If data-dense, give the headline in alt and move full values to an adjacent table\",\"Audit color: add a non-color cue wherever a distinction rides on color alone\",\"Run the cover test: hide the image and confirm the alt alone still teaches the point\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is \"more alt text is better alt text.\" Long, exhaustive alt is itself a barrier, because screen-reader output is linear and unskimmable, so a 200-word alt forces the learner to listen through everything to find the point; brevity plus an adjacent data table serves them far better. The second misreading is \"passing the checker means it is accessible.\" Automated tools verify that alt exists and flag a few color-contrast ratios, but they cannot tell whether the alt carries the instructional function or whether a distinction depends on color, the two failures that matter most. Human judgment, not the checker, is the standard.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An automated checker passes a bar chart whose alt reads 'a colorful bar chart with blue, green, and orange bars.' Why is the frame still inaccessible in practice?\" explanation=\"The checker only verifies that alt exists and is non-empty; it cannot judge function. The alt describes appearance, so a screen-reader user learns no ranking or result, and the series are distinguished by color alone, so a color-blind learner cannot tell them apart either. Passing the checker is not the goal; delivering the instructional meaning through a non-visual path is.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is accessible, because the checker passed and alt is present\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The alt describes appearance not function, and color-only coding fails color-blind learners, neither of which the checker can detect\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The only problem is that the alt is too short\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk a real frame with mixed images. The frame has a decorative banner photo of a lab, a meaningful line graph of reaction rate versus temperature, and a small \"click here\" arrow icon. Classify each: the banner is decorative (`alt=\"\"`), the graph is meaningful, the arrow is functional UI whose alt should name its action (\"expand the data table\"), not its shape. Write the graph's alt to function: \"reaction rate rises slowly up to 40C, then climbs sharply, doubling between 40 and 60C,\" with the precise values placed in a table beside it. Audit color: the graph plotted two reactions in red and green only, so add direct end-of-line labels so the two curves are distinguishable without color.\n\nApply the cover test: hide the graph and read the alt plus the table, a non-visual learner gets the trend and the numbers; a color-blind learner reading the visible graph gets the labels. The walkthrough shows the three decisions are separable: classification fixed the banner, function fixed the alt, and the color audit fixed a defect the alt alone would have missed.\n\nWork the three images on this frame one decision at a time.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Classifying and writing alt for a mixed frame\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The frame opens with a decorative banner photo of a lab. What alt should it carry?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A full description of the lab scene\",\"Empty alt so the screen reader skips it\",\"The word decorative so the learner knows to ignore it\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"A decorative image takes empty alt. Announcing ’decorative lab photo’ is noise that wastes a non-visual learner’s linear attention; empty alt lets the reader skip it.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The meaningful line graph plots reaction rate versus temperature. Which alt carries its function?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A red and green line chart on a white grid\",\"Reaction rate rises slowly to 40C, then climbs sharply, doubling between 40 and 60C\",\"A graph with two curves and an x-axis\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Function over appearance: the alt must deliver the trend the learner should take away, not the colors. Precise values go in an adjacent table so the alt stays brief.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The color audit then catches what the alt alone would miss: the two reactions were told apart by red and green only, so add direct end-of-line labels. Color blindness no longer hides the distinction, and the meaning survives grayscale.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead every image in the chapter twice, once for classification and once for the cover test. For each, decide decorative or meaningful and predict the alt it should carry; then imagine the image hidden and ask whether the alt alone would teach the point. A strong reader catches the two silent failures: alt that describes appearance instead of function, and a distinction that quietly depends on color. Keep a note of which images would need an adjacent table because their data is too dense for alt to carry.\n\nA strong reader of accessibility can, for any image, state its classification, the function its alt must deliver, where the full data lives if alt cannot hold it, and whether any meaning rides on color.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact reaches the stronger standard when the trace shows the classify-function-color sequence applied per image, including at least one image correctly marked decorative with empty alt and one meaningful image whose alt was rewritten from appearance to function. It records the cover test being run, and where data was dense, names the adjacent table or long description. Where an AI tool generated an exhaustive paragraph-length alt, the trace records trimming it to the headline and moving detail to a table.\n\nA strong closing note reads: \"I reclassified the lab banner as decorative and gave it empty alt so screen readers skip it, and I rewrote the chart alt from a color description to the ranking and the eight-point gap it teaches. I added on-bar value labels so the comparison survives color blindness, and put the full values in a table beside the chart. I rejected the tool's 200-word alt because linear audio makes exhaustive alt a barrier, not a help.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove from a static image to a data dashboard the learner can filter and re-sort interactively. Alt text alone cannot describe a display that changes on interaction, so the obligation transfers but the mechanism does not: accessibility now means a keyboard-operable interface, programmatic labels that update as the data changes, and a downloadable data table as the reliable equivalent. The transfer test is whether you can carry the function-not-appearance principle into a dynamic artifact where the \"image\" is never fixed. If your accessibility skill only knows how to write one alt string for one static picture, it will not survive the move to interactive media; the durable skill is guaranteeing a non-visual path to the same meaning, whatever form the visual takes.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/03-05-alt-text-and-accessibility-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"image accessibility-treatment trace\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"03.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 03.05. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_alt_text_and_accessibility\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes records cognitive-load diagnosis, support request, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect accessibility to the storyboarding work, the evidence-based revision that runs the cover test, and the bounded AI support whose verbose alt you must trim.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation\",\"accessibility\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- W3C (2023). Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2. W3C Recommendation. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n- CAST (2024). Universal Design for Learning Guidelines version 3.0. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_5_alt_text_and_accessibility_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_alt_text_and_accessibility","stem":"An instructional bar chart compares test scores across three teaching methods. Which alt text best serves a screen-reader user?","options":["A colorful bar chart with blue, green, and orange bars on a white background.","Bar chart: scores were highest for inquiry (82), then lecture (74), then lab (70), showing inquiry led by 8 points.","An image of a chart.","Chart.png, 1200 by 800 pixels."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Alt text conveys the image's instructional function, the comparison and its takeaway, not its appearance or file metadata.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_5_alt_describes_appearance","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["accessibility"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_5_alt_text_and_accessibility_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_alt_text_and_accessibility","stem":"A page has a decorative corner flourish and a labeled circuit diagram that learners must read. How should their alt attributes differ?","options":["Both should get the same generic alt text.","The flourish gets empty alt so screen readers skip it; the circuit diagram gets alt that carries its components and connections.","The flourish needs a long description; the circuit diagram can be left with empty alt.","Neither needs alt text."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Classify first. Decorative images take empty alt to avoid noise; meaningful images need alt that conveys their content. Reversing this is a common error.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_5_decorative_needs_no_alt","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["accessibility"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_5_alt_text_and_accessibility_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_alt_text_and_accessibility","stem":"An AI tool generates a status legend that marks safe steps green and risky steps red, with no other label. Before shipping, what accessibility judgment must you apply?","options":["Accept it, since red and green are intuitive.","Recognize that color-only coding excludes color-blind learners; add text labels or patterns so the safe-versus-risky distinction survives without color.","Make the red brighter so it stands out more.","Reject all AI-generated legends."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Meaning must not be carried by color alone. Adding labels or patterns keeps the distinction usable in color blindness and grayscale, which the tool will not enforce for you.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_5_color_alone_carries_meaning","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["accessibility"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_5_alt_text_and_accessibility_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_alt_text_and_accessibility","stem":"A multi-series line graph with twelve data points per series is too rich to capture in a short alt attribute. What is the best accessible solution?","options":["Write a single 250-word alt attribute containing every value.","Give a brief alt naming the graph's purpose and trend, plus a nearby data table or long description holding the full values.","Leave the alt empty, since the graph is complex.","Replace the graph with the word graph."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Alt text should stay brief. Complex visuals pair a short alt with a longer description or data table the learner can read in full, rather than overloading the alt attribute.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_5_overlay_alt_for_complex","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["accessibility"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"88cf881392cb5b403f0960b4bc45598ab80d2e2664b671ebcbeeb6486bf9bbee","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}